QNX defines TIOCPKT but devc-pty does not implement it.

vte_pty_new_sync() fails on QNX with:

    Failed to open PTY: Inappropriate I/O control operation

fd_set_cpkt() enables pty packet mode under #if defined(TIOCPKT), and
treats failure as fatal.  QNX's <sys/ioctl.h> does define TIOCPKT --
    #define TIOCPKT  _IOW('t', 112, int)   /* pty: set/clear packet mode */
-- but its devc-pty resource manager does not implement the request and
returns ENOTTY.  A macro being defined is not evidence that the kernel
supports it.

Ignoring the ENOTTY would be worse than the failure.  vte.cc's reader is
compiled under the same #if and consumes a leading packet-header byte from
every read(); with packet mode off but that branch compiled in, the first
byte of real terminal output is eaten and misread as a header.  The two
sites have to agree, so this turns both off together on QNX and the plain
read() path already present as the #else is used instead.  What is lost is
^S/^Q flow-control notification, not correctness.

Upstream-Status: QNX-specific

--- a/src/pty.cc
+++ b/src/pty.cc
@@ -340,11 +340,28 @@
         return false;
 }
 
+/* QNX's <sys/ioctl.h> defines TIOCPKT, but devc-pty does not implement it:
+ * the ioctl returns ENOTTY ("Inappropriate I/O control operation").  A macro
+ * being defined is not evidence the kernel supports the request.
+ *
+ * This has to be decided at compile time and identically in pty.cc and vte.cc.
+ * pty.cc treats a failed TIOCPKT as fatal, so vte_pty_new_sync() fails outright
+ * -- but simply ignoring that error would be worse: vte.cc's reader is compiled
+ * under the same #if and consumes a leading packet-header byte from every
+ * read().  With packet mode off but that branch compiled in, the first byte of
+ * real terminal output would be eaten and misread as a header.  Both sites must
+ * agree, so VTE_USE_TIOCPKT turns the pair off together and the plain read()
+ * path (the #else below) is used instead.
+ */
+#if defined(TIOCPKT) && !defined(__QNX__)
+# define VTE_USE_TIOCPKT 1
+#endif
+
 static int
 fd_set_cpkt(vte::libc::FD& fd)
 {
         auto ret = 0;
-#if defined(TIOCPKT)
+#if defined(VTE_USE_TIOCPKT)
         /* tty_ioctl(4) -> every read() gives an extra byte at the beginning
          * notifying us of stop/start (^S/^Q) events. */
         int one = 1;
